This version follows the pattern from left to right. The state `(i, j)` means that the remaining text starts at `i` and the remaining pattern starts at `j`.

For a normal character or `?`, both indexes move. A `*` gives two choices: skip it, or let it consume one character while keeping the pattern index in place. The cache prevents the same pair of indexes from being solved repeatedly.

For `str = "abc"` and `pat = "a*c"`, the state after matching `a` reaches the star. It can skip the star, which fails on `b`, or consume `b` and then try `c`. The second choice succeeds.

```python
class Solution:
    def wildCard(self, str: str, pat: str) -> bool:
        text_length = len(str)
        pattern_length = len(pat)
        memo = {}

        def all_stars(start: int) -> bool:
            for index in range(start, pattern_length):
                if pat[index] != '*':
                    return False
            return True

        def matches(text_index: int, pattern_index: int) -> bool:
            state = (text_index, pattern_index)
            if state in memo:
                return memo[state]

            if pattern_index == pattern_length:
                answer = text_index == text_length
            elif text_index == text_length:
                answer = all_stars(pattern_index)
            elif pat[pattern_index] == '*':
                skip_star = matches(text_index, pattern_index + 1)
                use_star = matches(text_index + 1, pattern_index)
                answer = skip_star or use_star
            elif (pat[pattern_index] == '?'
                  or pat[pattern_index] == str[text_index]):
                answer = matches(text_index + 1, pattern_index + 1)
            else:
                answer = False

            memo[state] = answer
            return answer

        return matches(0, 0)
```

Time complexity: `O(n * m)`, with each text and pattern index pair cached once.
Space complexity: `O(n * m)` for the cache, plus up to `O(n + m)` recursion stack space. The input strings are not changed.

I kept the matching greedy by remembering the latest `*`. At first, a star matches an empty part. If a later character fails, the star is allowed to cover one more character, and matching continues after the star.

The final loop is important because the remaining pattern can only contain stars if the match is valid.

Dry run for `str = "adceb"`, `pat = "*a*b"`: the first star starts at the beginning, `a` matches, and the second star first covers nothing. When `d`, `c`, and `e` do not match `b`, the second star expands until `b` can match.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean wildCard(String str, String pat) {
        int textIndex = 0;
        int patternIndex = 0;
        int lastStar = -1;
        int starMatchStart = 0;

        while (textIndex < str.length()) {
            if (patternIndex < pat.length()
                    && (pat.charAt(patternIndex) == '?'
                    || pat.charAt(patternIndex) == str.charAt(textIndex))) {
                textIndex++;
                patternIndex++;
            } else if (patternIndex < pat.length()
                    && pat.charAt(patternIndex) == '*') {
                lastStar = patternIndex;
                starMatchStart = textIndex;
                patternIndex++;
            } else if (lastStar != -1) {
                patternIndex = lastStar + 1;
                starMatchStart++;
                textIndex = starMatchStart;
            } else {
                return false;
            }
        }

        while (patternIndex < pat.length() && pat.charAt(patternIndex) == '*') {
            patternIndex++;
        }

        return patternIndex == pat.length();
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(str.length() * pat.length())` in the worst case because of backtracking.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input strings are not changed.

Instead of storing a full text by pattern table, this uses a set of possible pattern positions. A star can move past itself without consuming a character, so an `epsilon_closure` function keeps adding the positions reachable through stars.

For each text character, normal characters and `?` move to the next pattern position. A `*` stays at its own position because it can consume another character. At the end, the pattern is accepted only if its final position is reachable.

Dry run for `str = "ab"`, `pat = "a*"`: position `0` matches `a`, then the closure includes both the star position and the end position. The star can consume `b`, and the end position remains reachable.

```python
class Solution:
    def wildCard(self, str: str, pat: str) -> bool:
        pattern_length = len(pat)

        def epsilon_closure(positions):
            reachable = set(positions)
            changed = True

            while changed:
                changed = False
                for position in list(reachable):
                    if (position < pattern_length
                            and pat[position] == '*'
                            and position + 1 not in reachable):
                        reachable.add(position + 1)
                        changed = True

            return reachable

        current_positions = epsilon_closure({0})

        for character in str:
            next_positions = set()

            for position in current_positions:
                if position == pattern_length:
                    continue

                pattern_char = pat[position]
                if pattern_char == '*':
                    next_positions.add(position)
                elif pattern_char == '?' or pattern_char == character:
                    next_positions.add(position + 1)

            current_positions = epsilon_closure(next_positions)
            if not current_positions:
                return False

        return pattern_length in epsilon_closure(current_positions)
```

Time complexity: `O(n * m)` in the worst case, where `n` is the text length and `m` is the pattern length.
Space complexity: `O(m)` for the reachable pattern positions. The input strings are not changed.

The recursive version is a direct check of the matching rules. When the current pattern character is `*`, one call skips the star and another call lets it take the current text character. For `?` and equal characters, both positions move together.

A non-empty text cannot match an empty pattern. If the text is finished first, every remaining pattern character must be `*`.

This is useful for seeing the choices clearly, but it can repeat many states. For long inputs with several stars, the memoized or table-based versions are safer.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean wildCard(String str, String pat) {
        return matches(str, pat, 0, 0);
    }

    private boolean matches(String str, String pat, int textIndex, int patternIndex) {
        if (patternIndex == pat.length()) {
            return textIndex == str.length();
        }

        if (textIndex == str.length()) {
            for (int index = patternIndex; index < pat.length(); index++) {
                if (pat.charAt(index) != '*') {
                    return false;
                }
            }
            return true;
        }

        char patternChar = pat.charAt(patternIndex);

        if (patternChar == '*') {
            return matches(str, pat, textIndex, patternIndex + 1)
                    || matches(str, pat, textIndex + 1, patternIndex);
        }

        if (patternChar == '?'
                || patternChar == str.charAt(textIndex)) {
            return matches(str, pat, textIndex + 1, patternIndex + 1);
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

For edge cases, `str = ""` matches `pat = "***"`, but it does not match `pat = "*a"`. Also, any non-empty text fails when the pattern is empty.

Time complexity: exponential in the worst case because the star can create two recursive branches.
Space complexity: `O(n + m)` for the recursion stack. The input strings are not changed.

A prefix DP table makes the two meanings of `*` easy to store. `dp[i][j]` says whether the first `i` characters of `str` match the first `j` characters of `pat`.

A normal match or `?` uses the diagonal cell. For `*`, the star either matches nothing, using the left cell, or consumes one text character, using the cell above. The first row also handles patterns such as `***`, which match an empty string.

Edge cases covered here include an empty text, an empty pattern, and a pattern made only of stars.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    bool wildCard(string str, string pat) {
        int textLength = static_cast<int>(str.size());
        int patternLength = static_cast<int>(pat.size());

        vector<vector<bool>> dp(
            textLength + 1,
            vector<bool>(patternLength + 1, false)
        );

        dp[0][0] = true;

        for (int j = 1; j <= patternLength; ++j) {
            if (pat[j - 1] == '*') {
                dp[0][j] = dp[0][j - 1];
            }
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= textLength; ++i) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= patternLength; ++j) {
                char patternChar = pat[j - 1];

                if (patternChar == '*') {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j] || dp[i][j - 1];
                } else if (patternChar == '?' || patternChar == str[i - 1]) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[textLength][patternLength];
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n * m)`, where `n` is the text length and `m` is the pattern length.
Space complexity: `O(n * m)`. The input strings are not modified.
